    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/8/88428.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 39,
    "turn_text": "Unfortunately, I don't think it has a chance of getting passed in this Congress, but I do think it has a chance of getting passed in the next Congress. And I do think we still have a shot of getting a smaller version through, but it's going to be tough. And this is a bill that I worked on as soon as COVID hit, because I saw countries like Germany and countries in Europe already starting to put something similar in place. And I started working with economists, Joseph Stiglitz, Nobel Prize-winning economist, Mark Zandi, who's the head of Moody's. He's the chief economist at Moody's, not exactly a progressive but super smart, wonderful man. And a number of others, the Congressional Progressive Caucus Center, was really crucial in this as well. in really looking at how would the United States of America do something similar where the federal government would essentially guarantee support to businesses to keep workers in their jobs, getting their paychecks and their benefits, and subsidize some of the operating costs for businesses to stay in operation. It would be for all businesses, you know, unlike the paycheck protection program, which is a PPP program where all these loans went, had to go through banks. And we know that big banks have never been the friends of small business and particularly minority businesses. So many of those businesses got left out big companies that had were friends of the trumps or had relationships with big banks they got loans, but many of them didn't use the money to keep people in their paychecks and so the paycheck guarantee was a completely different idea was you know subsidize people to stay in their payrolls. and their benefits. They wouldn't lose their health care because they lost their job as has happened to 27 million Americans so far. But also we would get all these small businesses because they would get money directly from Treasury. Everyone has to file a tax return every quarter no matter how big or small your business is. And it's just a really easy way to get money to people and to bring certainty to businesses and to workers to be able to continue. Now it would also keep unemployment down you know we went the route of expanding unemployment which is great i totally support that but think about what happens when everyone moves into the unemployment system these unemployment systems are collapsing because there's too many systems that have been decimated because of austerity spending not enough money being put into those systems they can't handle these huge numbers and then some states like florida and texas have gone out of their way to really skinny down their unemployment benefits so that a lot of the lowest wage workers don't even qualify for unemployment, which is just crazy. And what happens in a recession? The people that have the hardest time getting out of unemployment and back into jobs? Guess who? BIPOC folks, black, indigenous, and folks of color because of the hiring discrimination that exists. If we had done a Paycheck Recovery Act plus expanded unemployment, we would have kept a lot of people in their jobs. Almost every other major country, this pure country in the world, has done this. Malaysia, Singapore, Taiwan, Australia, UK, France, Germany, et cetera. But the United States has not. So it's a big idea, but it's really essential. And frankly, it would have saved us a ton of money because we wouldn't be doing very expensive cobra subsidies. We wouldn't be pushing more people on to Medicaid. We wouldn't need to do stimulus checks or you could target stimulus checks to those who are most vulnerable instead of doing a $1,200 check to everyone. There are just all kinds of ways in which I think we actually would have saved money if we had had the foresight to just be big and bold like that.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
