    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/8/88428.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 43,
    "turn_text": "I don't think that they're not complimentary. I think you can talk about it. Like I've said, one of the ways I talked to my colleagues about it is I said, listen, this is a pro-jobs bill. Democrats should be the party of keeping people in their jobs, not sending them on to unemployment. And I don't think that that counters in any way the idea that we should have a strong unemployment system so that if we can't keep people in their jobs, if they have conditions that make them even more vulnerable and they end up unemployed, then we should be really generous. But how about the fact that we should do both, right? And people love that. And actually, after we passed the Heroes Act, which I ended up voting against, which was not the common thing that Democrats did, because it didn't include this, and I also thought it didn't include enough for health care specifically. Josh Hawley, who's a conservative senator from Missouri, told the press or released a statement like, I can't believe the Democrats didn't want to be the party of keeping people in their jobs by passing the paycheck",
